Results. Healthy subjects. By comparing both latency and am-
plitude values obtained with neck in neutral position and neck
flexed, statistical analysis did not reveal any difference (Student
t-tests: p /H110220.05). In particular, the N13 amplitude was always
within normal limits in both conditions (median nerve SEPs,
mean N13/N9 ratio: 1.58 with the neck in neutral position, 1.6
with neck flexed; ulnar nerve SEPs, mean N13/N9 ratio: 1.37 with
the neck in neutral position, 1.5 with neck flexed).
Patients with ALS. By comparing both latency and amplitude
values obtained with neck in neutral position and neck flexed,
statistical analysis did not reveal any difference (Student t-test:
p /H110220.05). In particular, the N13 amplitude was always within
normal limits in both conditions (median nerve SEPs, mean
N13/N9 ratio: 1.59 with the neck in neutral position, 1.3 with neck
flexed; ulnar nerve SEPs, mean N13/N9 ratio: 1.15 with the neck
in neutral position, 1.14 with neck flexed).
Patients with Hirayama disease. Baseline recordings (neck in
neutral position) did not show any abnormality of brachial plexus
N9 and of scalp-recorded P14 and N20 responses. The cervical
N13 was abnormally reduced after right ulnar nerve stimulation
in Patient 3 and after left ulnar nerve stimulation in Patient 5.
Recordings with neck flexed did not show any change of either
latency or amplitude values of the brachial plexus N9 and of
scalp-recorded P14 and N20 responses (Student t-test: p /H110220.05).
By contrast the N13 amplitude was different between the two
conditions (Student t-test: median nerve, p /H110050.024; ulnar nerve,
p /H110050.013). During neck flexion, the N13 response was abnormal
in all patients after ulnar nerve stimulation and in four of five
patients after median nerve stimulation. SEP amplitude values
from our patients are illustrated in table 2. The figure illustrates
SEPs from one patient with Hirayama disease.
Discussion. The main finding of the present study
is that neck flexion always caused a significant re-
duction of the cervical N13 response in patients with
Hirayama disease, more evident after stimulation of
the ulnar nerve, whereas it did not cause any re-
markable SEP change in our control subjects or in
patients with ALS. We can exclude that the afore-
mentioned N13 abnormality can be merely due to a
displacement of its generator during neck flexion,
because such a mechanisms should be present also
in control subjects. We cannot fully exclude that an
abnormal susceptibility to the neck flexion of cord
neurons affected by a degenerative disease can ac-
count for the aforementioned N13 abnormality; how-
ever, patients with ALS did not show any SEP
change during neck flexion. Thus, the present study
seems to confirm that neck flexion plays a major role
in the pathophysiology of Hirayama disease.
We did not perform cervical cord MRI during neck
flexion in our patients; thus, we cannot speculate
whether this specific position-related sensitivity of
the cervical cord is caused by cervical cord compres-
sion during neck flexion. Concerning this point, the
earlier literature is conflicting. Neuroradiologic find-
ings very similar to those described by Hirayama et
al. have also been found in healthy subjects.
3 More-
over, other authors reported that MRI during neck
flexion often did not show cord compression during
neck flexion.
3,4 It is possible that, at least in these
patients, a slight cord displacement could lead to
microcirculatory changes sufficient to cause a signif-
icant involvement of the central gray matter.
7,8,10
In more advanced phases of disease, both MRI
and SEPs can demonstrate a stabilized involvement
of the cervical central gray matter. In our series,
only two patients showed clear abnormalities of the
cervical cord MRI. In both patients, baseline SEPs
showed an evident abnormality of the N13 after
stimulation of the ulnar nerve, thus revealing a sta-
ble and chronic dysfunction of the cervical gray mat-
