Andrew D. Pinto, BSc; Anthony E. Lang, MD, FRCPC; and Robert Chen, MBBChir, MSc, FRCPC
Abstract—The cerebellum is implicated in the pathogenesis of essential tremor (ET). The authors examined the excitabil-
ity of the cerebellothalamocortical pathway in ET and found it to be normal. Tremor reset occurred with motor cortex
stimulation but not with cerebellar stimulation. These findings suggest that the abnormal oscillatory activity in ET does
not originate in the cerebellar cortex but are consistent with the abnormal activity being transmitted through the
cerebellothalamocortical pathway being driven by abnormal cerebellar afferent input from areas such as the inferior olive.
NEUROLOGY 2003;60:1985–1987
Essential tremor (ET), characterized by a 4- to 12-Hz
postural and kinetic tremor, is probably the common-
est movement disorder in adults. Gait abnormalities,
prolonged reaction time, and kinetic analysis
1 in ET
are consistent with involvement of the cerebellum. 2
PET studies have suggested a central oscillator in-
volving the cerebellum and red nucleus,
3 and repeti-
tive transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) of the
cerebellum has been reported to transiently improve
ET.
4 As thalamotomy and deep brain stimulation
(DBS) of the ventral intermediate (VIM) nucleus (cere-
bellar receiving area) of the thalamus are effective
treatments for ET, it may be related to abnormal neu-
ronal discharges in the cerebellothalamocortical path-
way.
2
TMS can be used to study the cerebellothalamo-
cortical pathway. 5,6 Cerebellar stimulation inhibits
the contralateral motor cortex 5 to 7 milliseconds
later.
5 This inhibition is absent in patients with le-
sions of the cerebellum or along the cerebellotha-
lamic pathway and in patients with cerebellar
atrophy but is preserved in patients with lesions in
the afferent pathways to the cerebellum.
7 Cerebellar
stimulation likely activates Purkinje cells in the cer-
ebellar cortex, which inhibits the excitatory connec-
tion from the dentate and interposed nuclei to VIM
thalamus, resulting in reduced excitation from the
VIM to the motor cortex. In the current study, we
tested the cerebellothalamocortical pathway in ET
patients. If cerebellum is involved in ET, the inhibi-
tory effect of cerebellum stimulation on the motor
cortex may be decreased.
Methods. We examined 9 ET patients (5 women; ages 53 /H1100616
[SD] years, range 21 to 72 years) and 10 normal volunteers (3
women; ages 49 /H1100619 years, range 26 to 81 years). Patients were
diagnosed using established criteria 8 and discontinued their med-
ications for 24 hours. Written informed consent was obtained, and
the protocol was approved by the University Health Network Re-
