search Ethics Board. Tremor was scored by two independent as-
sessors using the Fahn–Tolosa–Marin tremor rating scale. 9
The cerebellothalamocortical pathway was tested in a paired
TMS protocol as previously described. 6 Surface electromyography
(EMG) was recorded from the right first dorsal interosseous (FDI)
muscle. A double-cone coil (110-mm mean diameter; Magstim
Company, Whitland, UK) was used for cerebellar stimulation and
was set at 5% below the threshold for pyramidal tract activation.
The motor cortex was stimulated with a flat figure-of-eight coil
(70-mm mean diameter). Right cerebellar stimulation was the
conditioning stimulus, and left motor cortex stimulation was the
test stimulus. Interstimulus intervals (ISI) of 3 to 9 and 15 milli-
seconds were tested. Each run consisted of 10 trials of each ISI
and 10 trials with the test pulse alone delivered in random order.
The four conditions tested were 1) target muscle at rest, test
stimulus alone inducing motor-evoked potentials (MEP) of about
0.5 mV (rest 0.5 mV); 2) target muscle at rest, test stimulus alone
inducing MEP of about 2 mV (rest 2 mV); 3) right FDI muscle
activated to 10% maximum voluntary contraction, test stimulus
alone inducing MEP of about 2 mV (active 2 mV); and 4) subject
holding the right arm outstretched with the hand relaxed, test
stimulus alone inducing MEP of about 0.5 mV (arm stretch). Inhi-
bition or facilitation for each trial was expressed as a ratio to the
mean unconditioned MEP amplitude for each subject. Repeated
measures analysis of variance was performed, examining the ef-
fect of ISI and group on the time course of suppression.
To study tremor reset, EMG was recorded from the right FDI,
the wrist flexor muscle, and the extensor muscle for 5 seconds
before and after right cerebellar stimulation or left motor cortex
stimulation with the patient holding the right arm outstretched.
The cerebellar stimulus was set to 5% below the threshold for
pyramidal tract activation. Motor cortex stimulation induced
about 0.5-mV MEP in the resting right FDI muscle. Ten trials of
each stimulation were performed in random order. Tremor reset
was analyzed using a previously described method.
10 The average
cycle length was first calculated by measuring the peak times of
five tremor bursts immediately before the stimulus. Using the
time between the last tremor burst and the stimulus, we calcu-
lated the proportion of the average cycle that had elapsed (%cl)
and predicted when the five tremor bursts following the stimulus
should occur if there were no interruption. The difference between
the predicted and the actual peak times of the first five tremor
bursts (d1 to d5) following the stimulus was then plotted against
the %cl in each trial. Combining all the trials for a particular
paradigm, we created regression lines for each of the five graphs
(d1t od5). Resetting index was the average slope of these regres-
sion lines. A resetting index of 0 represents no reset, and 1 repre-
sents complete reset.
Results. Results are given as means /H11006SD. The tremor score for
ET patients was 48 /H1100615 (maximum score 144). The score for the
right arm only was 14 /H110065 (maximum score 32). There were no
